# Notification item

A single entry in the notification drawer: a type icon, a title, a
relative time and a clamped message, muted once read.

## When to use

- One row inside the header's notification drawer — `render_header`
  renders a list of these from its `notifications`.

## When not to use

- Page or data state — `alert` (persistent, in the page).
- Action outcomes — `toast` (transient, after a redirect).
- A standalone callout — this is a list row, not a card.

## Usage

`render_notification_item` (`Gesso::Components::NotificationItemHelper`) maps
the notification `type` to an icon and colour, and the `read` state to
its muting, then renders the row.

```erb
<%%= render_notification_item(notification: {
  title: "Report generated",
  message: "Your monthly report is ready for download.",
  type: "success",
  time: "30m ago",
  read: false
}) %>
```

| Key | Notes |
| --- | ----- |
| `title` | the headline |
| `message` | supporting text, clamped to two lines |
| `type` | see **Types** below |
| `time` | relative time label, e.g. `"5m ago"` |
| `read` | `true` mutes the row (read notifications recede) |

## Types

The `type` sets the leading icon and its colour; an unknown or missing
type falls back to `info`.

<%= embed :notification_item, :variants %>

| Type | When to use |
| ---- | ----------- |
| `info` (default) | Neutral, incidental news — a new version, a tip. |
| `success` | Something the user set in motion has completed — a report is ready. |
| `warning` | Something needs the user's attention but isn't an error. |

## Do and don't

- Do write the title as the outcome or event, with the detail in the
  message — the title is what's scanned.
- Do pick `type` by meaning, matching the alert/toast colour rules.
- Don't put actions in a notification item; it announces, it doesn't
  act. A notification that needs a decision belongs in the page.
- Don't rely on colour alone — the title carries the meaning, the icon
  and colour reinforce it.

## Accessibility and content

- The icon is decorative beside the title; meaning is in the text.
- Read state is conveyed by muting *and* stays in the DOM, so the
  history is available to assistive tech.
